\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The counting pairing meets only at the complement}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring, and keep the torus, the
coboundary, and the counting operator #{\extD} of \ref{fdip-00OL}.
Let #{M} be any set of cubes, and write #{\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_M}
for the counting sum over cubes in #{M}. Say that two cubes meet
when one appears in the cellular boundary of the other. Then
##{\langle \extD\alpha,\beta\rangle_M-\langle\alpha,\extD\beta\rangle_M
=\langle\alpha,\extD\beta\rangle_{X\setminus M}
-\langle \extD\alpha,\beta\rangle_{X\setminus M}.}
Either side is unchanged if #{\alpha} and #{\beta} are changed
off the cubes that lie in #{M} or meet a cube of #{M}.}

\proof{
\p{The counting pairing of \ref{fdip-00OL} makes #{\extdag} the
adjoint of #{\covwedge}. The form is symmetric, so #{\extD} is self-adjoint
on the whole torus:
##{\langle \extD\alpha,\beta\rangle_X=\langle\alpha,\extD\beta\rangle_X.}
Split each sum into the cubes in #{M} and the cubes outside
#{M}. Rearrangement is the displayed identity.}

\p{For a cube #{c} in #{M}, the formula of \ref{fdip-00OK} writes
#{(\extD\alpha)(c)} as a sum of values of #{\alpha} on cubes that
meet #{c}. The same holds for #{\beta}. The left-hand side sums
only over cubes in #{M}, so it reads #{\alpha} and #{\beta} only
on cubes in #{M} and on cubes that meet them. The two sides are
equal, so the right-hand side is unchanged by the same
alteration.}
}
